import Blanc.ExecutionReachable
import Blanc.ExecutionTraceSystem

/-!
# The canonical system-contract code

The four contracts a block's system messages call: the EIP-4788 beacon-roots contract, the EIP-2935
history-storage contract, the EIP-7002 withdrawal-request contract and the EIP-7251
consolidation-request contract.

**Provenance.**  The bytes below are the `code` fields of the four entries of
`scripts/t8n/amsterdam-system-alloc.json` in Jaune at the pinned commit
`dc21abb04c885f9660072aa634ab8a028703eee6`, at the four addresses
`beaconRootsAddress`, `historyStorageAddress`, `withdrawalRequestPredeployAddress` and
`consolidationRequestPredeployAddress` (Jaune's `Machine.lean`), which are the addresses
`processUncheckedSystemTransaction` is called with in Jaune's `applyBody` and
`processGeneralPurposeRequests`.  Each length and SHA-256 is recorded on its definition.  This
module proves properties of those bytes only; that a real chain holds them at those addresses is
the premise a consumer states (`SystemCodeInstalled`).

**Why the reachability-aware notion.**  The EIP-7002 code has `0xF4` (`DELEGATECALL`) bytes at
offsets `67`, `123`, `128` and `141`, all inside `PUSH` immediates, so `SpawnFree` (which decodes
at every offset) is false of it (`withdrawalRequestCode_not_spawnFree`); `SpawnFreeReach` (no
spawning instruction at a position an execution can reach) holds of all four, by a kernel
decision of the linear walk `spawnFreeCheck`.
-/

namespace Blanc

open Jaune

